// In `g`, `A is P` has two witnesses: the requirement of `g` (nearer) and the declared conformance
// (X = B). `f<A>` can only be satisfied with the declared one (B is Q; nothing is known about the
// X of the requirement). The result of `f` is then a B, NOT the `A.X` of g's requirement.
// ILL TYPED: `let x: A.X = ...` where A.X is selected through g's requirement receives a B.
// (If `A.X` in the body of g is resolved through the declared conformance instead, the program is
// well typed; the control b02c makes the same test with an explicit B.)
trait P { type X
  fun get() -> X }
trait Q {}
struct A { public memberwise init }
struct B { public memberwise init }
given B is Q {}
given A is P { type X = B
  fun get() -> B { B() } }
fun f<T where T is P, T.X is Q>(t: T) -> T.X { t.get() }
fun g<where A is P>(a: A, y: A.X) {
  var x = y
  x = f<A>(a)
}
